course: dictionary
lesson: 561
title: Checking words: audit
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An audit is a careful check. An official audit checks a company's or organisation's money records, to make sure they are correct and honest. You can also audit as a verb: to carry out such a check.
explain: Look at the words around the word. «The school did an energy audit to count the lights left on.» The lights and the counting tell you an audit is a careful check, here of how much power the school uses. Try the swap: "The school did a careful check to count the lights left on." It still makes sense, so your meaning is right.
explain: Brackets can give you the meaning too. In «recounts, audits (careful checks) and dozens of court cases did not find fraud», the words in brackets tell you audits means careful checks. Fraud means cheating, so the sentence says the checks found no cheating.
fact: An audit is a careful check.
fact: An official audit checks that money records are correct and honest.
fact: «Fraud» means cheating.
fact: In maths, an audit means checking that the numbers add up.
fact: Brackets often explain a hard word: «audits (careful checks)» tells you what audits means.
exercise: «The school did an energy audit to count the lights left on.» What does audit mean here? | *A careful check | A new paint colour | A loud bell | A school trip
exercise: «Audits (careful checks) found no cheating.» What do the brackets tell you audits means? | A kind of cheating | A court case | *Careful checks | A party
exercise: In maths, an audit checks that the numbers add up. What does audit mean in this sentence? | *To check the numbers are correct | To guess the answer | To lose count | To draw a graph
test: Which phrase means a check of how much power a building uses? | Safety bell | Paint colour | Court case | *Energy audit
test: Mia says audit is a kind of food. What is the correct meaning? | *A careful check | A song | A type of coat | A dessert
exam: Sam says an audit is a party for money. Which is right? | *A careful check to make sure records are correct and honest | A game of cards | A party with money games | A new bank account
